# **Kubernetes Fundamentals**

## **1. YAML File Structure**

| Field           | Description                              | Data Type            |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------- |
| apiVersion      | Kubernetes API version                   | String               |
| kind            | Resource type (Pod, Deployment, Service) | String               |
| metadata        | Resource information (name, labels)      | Dictionary           |
| spec            | Desired configuration of the resource    | Dictionary           |
| spec.containers | Containers inside the Pod                | List of dictionaries |

## **2. Create a Simple Nginx Pod**

**Filename: nginx.yaml**

apiVersion: v1\
&#x20;   kind: Pod

metadata:\
&#x20;     name: nginx-pod

spec:\
&#x20;     containers:\
&#x20;       \- name: nginx-container\
&#x20;         image: nginx

## **3. Create or Apply a Configuration**

kubectl apply -f nginx.yaml\
&#x20;   kubectl create -f nginx.yaml\
&#x20;   kubectl run nginx-pod --image=nginx

- **apply:** Create or update a resource.
- **create:** Create a new resource.
- **run:** Create a Pod directly without a YAML file.

## **4. View Resources**

kubectl cluster-info\
&#x20;   kubectl get nodes\
&#x20;   kubectl get pods\
&#x20;   kubectl get pods -A\
&#x20;   kubectl describe pod nginx-pod

- **-A:** Display resources from all namespaces.
- **describe:** Display detailed information about a resource.

## **5. Display Additional Information**

kubectl get pods -o wide\
&#x20;   kubectl get nodes -o wide\
&#x20;   kubectl get deployments -o wide\
&#x20;   kubectl get services -o wide

- **-o:** Output format.
- **wide:** Display additional columns.

## **6. Access a Running Container**

&#x20;   kubectl exec -it nginx-pod -- sh

Opens an interactive shell inside the container.

## **7. Delete Resources**

kubectl delete pod nginx-pod\
&#x20;   kubectl delete node worker1

- Deleting a Pod terminates its containers.
- Deleting a Node removes its Kubernetes resource, not the actual VM or physical machine.

## **8. Edit a Resource**

&#x20;   kubectl edit pod nginx-pod

Opens the existing Pod configuration in a text editor. Only supported fields can be modified.

## **9. Namespace Management**
A namespace is a logical grouping of resources within a Kubernetes cluster, similar to a folder.

kubectl get namespaces\
&#x20;   kubectl create namespace development\
&#x20;   kubectl get pods -n development\
&#x20;   kubectl get pods -A

- **-n:** Specify a namespace.
- **-A:** All namespaces.